WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.334
The key insight is that images are highly redundant.

00:00:03.414 --> 00:00:08.651
Patches repeat, textures recur, and similar
structures appear across many images.

00:00:08.731 --> 00:00:13.443
Classical representation learning, dictionary
learning and sparse coding,

00:00:13.523 --> 00:00:20.270
was built exactly to capture this: represent many
signals as sparse combinations of a shared dictionary.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:26.769
A handful of recent distillation methods started
exploiting relationships between synthetic images,

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:30.445
but none tackled redundancy in a spatially-agnostic way,

00:00:30.525 --> 00:00:32.676
no matter where a feature appears.

00:00:32.756 --> 00:00:40.094
SPEED asks: what if we spend almost none of the
storage budget on a shared dictionary and per-image sparse codes,

00:00:40.174 --> 00:00:43.902
and let a small network reconstruct rich images from them?

00:00:43.982 --> 00:00:46.790
That reframing is where the gains come from.
